    "created_at": "2025-09-22T16:41:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-09-22T16:41:04Z",
    "body": "The security issue from this kind of change stems from different parsers (especially in browser vs server-side sanitizer) disagree on the shape of the DOM tree. If there's an opt in to treat some tags as case-sensitive, then whether that is supported or not would give different DOM structures, which can be exploited to bypass sanitizers and execute script (mutation XSS or mXSS).\n\nI suppose the tag matching could still be done case-insensitively, but create the element based on the casing in the start tag. This doesn't have the mXSS issue, though I'm not certain there aren't other possible security issues.\n\nAs @annevk said, we try to avoid adding new mode switches to HTML parsing, or even try to reduce them (see #9426). As such I'm inclined to close this as wontfix, but we can keep it open for a bit.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
