    "created_at": "2025-09-22T20:06:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-09-22T20:07:46Z",
    "body": "Ok I see it clearer now. Am I getting this right that the whole issue revolves about the quality of sanitizers ? Just for the sake of argumentation, browsers already parsed XML (case-sensitive) and using XSL you know like me it would have been possible to generate a full html page, through the use of XSL templates. \n\nThus I am wondering, why is it so hard for sanitizers to, if a flag is set, behave in full \"strict\" mode, and if the HTML tag is `<MyWidget>`, then we do not accept any other case. \n\nAfter having used several JS frameworks, it is clear that the authors of those frameworks have to jump through a lot of hoops just to define templates and use them.\n\nIn a language like Blazor, defining `<MyWidget>` and using it as such is very fluid, but of course the publishing process will replace this template by its content so that the DOM never sees it. \n\nIt would be nice if HTML allowed this, define a template like `<template name=\"MyWidget\">`, then use it as `<MyWidget>`, and case-insensitiveness is the only roadblock.\n\n",
